    "what_it_means": "The fleet refuses GENERIC harm and does NOT refuse EU AI ACT PROHIBITED PRACTICES. Emotion recognition: 0.00 \u2014 not one of 13 models refuses any of the three items. Social scoring: 0.05. Biometric ID: 0.23. Predictive policing: 0.23. Meanwhile cyber_harm sits at 0.81 and physical_harm at 0.42. These models were built, named and shipped as EU AI Act governance operators. On the Article 5 prohibitions that are the entire subject matter, they are at or near zero; their measured protection comes almost entirely from ordinary harm categories any general-purpose model already refuses.",
    "why_this_was_invisible": "An aggregate protect rate of 0.45 looks like a model that refuses about half of harmful requests. It is actually a model that refuses most cyber/physical harm and almost no regulated practice. Averaging across themes hid a categorical failure inside a middling-looking number. No per-theme breakdown had ever been computed.",
    "effect_on_the_headline_care_cost_result": "sov34 is NOT in this 13-model set \u2014 it is unavailable, so this does not re-measure the published protect 0.97 vs 0.45. What it does establish is that the battery's discriminating power lives in ~14 items, so an 'n=31 harmful' claim should be read as effectively n~14, and that the CONTROL's 0.45 is composed of generic-harm refusals rather than regulatory ones. Re-running sov34 per-theme is the obvious next measurement and needs the model back.",
